The audio analysis (crates/audio) implements the frequency analysis described for
the AnalyserNode in the Web Audio API specification (W3C, https://www.w3.org/TR/webaudio/):
Blackman window, magnitude scaling, smoothing and the decibel-to-byte conversion.


5. Person segmentation model (downloaded at run time, not part of this repository)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Remove Background (crates/seg) downloads and runs MediaPipe Selfie Segmentation
(landscape variant), Google LLC, Apache License 2.0 (full text in section 8).
Model card: https://storage.googleapis.com/mediapipe-assets/Model%20Card%20MediaPipe%20Selfie%20Segmentation.pdf
ONNX conversion by the onnx-community (Apache-2.0), fetched from
https://huggingface.co/onnx-community/mediapipe_selfie_segmentation_landscape
The file is verified by its SHA-256 before use. It is inference only: the network
runs through tract (MIT OR Apache-2.0, https://github.com/sonos/tract).


6. Rust dependencies
--------------------

Crates pulled in through Cargo.toml keep their own licenses; run
`cargo tree` or a tool such as cargo-about to list them.

Overlay clips (assets/video): generated by crates/gpu/examples/gen_overlays.rs,
no third-party material. The app icon (assets/icon) is generated by
crates/gpu/examples/gen_icon.rs, also without third-party material.

Bundled ffmpeg (Windows and macOS downloads only)
    The Windows and macOS downloads contain unmodified builds of the ffmpeg and
    ffprobe programs (FFmpeg 9.0.2, https://ffmpeg.org), licensed under the GNU
    General Public License version 3 (they include GPL components such as
    libx264). They are separate programs that phthora starts; phthora itself is
    not linked against them. Corresponding source code: https://ffmpeg.org and
    the build recipes below.
    Windows: https://github.com/BtbN/FFmpeg-Builds, release
             autobuild-2026-09-30-13-08, win64-gpl-shared-9.0 (its LICENSE.txt
             is included next to the programs)
    macOS:   https://ffmpeg.martin-riedl.de, release builds 9.0.2 for arm64 and
             x86_64 (https://gitlab.com/martinr92/ffmpeg), joined into one
             universal program
    The Linux downloads do not contain ffmpeg; it comes from the distribution.


